{1720}{171 
 Eons ago, before the Age of Legends itself, there was an ancient clan of Dwarves that practised the art of Magicke. Their leader was Hourad Mithrilstone, ancestor to Jormund Mithrilstone and cousin to Durin, founder of the first Dwarven clans.
After many years of traversing the land of Arcanum, the Magicke clan split into three factions and decided to go their seperate ways.
One of them settled near an Elven tribe, which was soon to become Quintarra when the great Nasrudin became head of the Arcanum Council, and made the Elves a much stronger race. Their, they bound their Magickes with the roots of trees and the soil of the ground. They became known as the Clan Of Fertile Earth.}
{1721}{171 
The second clan settled on a mass of land in the midst of a flowing river. The land there was soon to become Tarant, the largest city in Arcanum. Their clan became known as the Clan Of Flatlands, for there were no mountains in the area, and they were the only Dwarven clan ever to venture so far away from the mountains.
The final clan settled at the southernmost point in Arcanum, and there they used their Magicke to help them communicate with beasts of the sea. They lay their powers in the Water, and crafted many great ships which could power through tidal waves like knives through warm butter. They became known as the Clan Of The Sea, and they remained at the southern shores for a long time.
}
{1722}{171 
 Although these three factions had split, they decided that they still needed to keep in contact with one another. Such things as teleportation spells were insufficient and tedious forms of travel. After making journies via the teleport spell, many of their mages that it was tiresome to perform such a spell over so vast a range.
So, each tribe crafted almighty stones of transportational Magicke, each of them larger than the cities that stand in their places today. They were required to be large so that they could teleport vast numbers of Dwarves at one time.
Eventually Arronax saw these Dwarves as a threat to him, and he also dissaproved of the fact that Dwarves could use Magicke. He destroyed the stones and smote the clans with his armies of Dark Elves.
}
{1723}{171 
 However, Arronax could not destroy the rune cores set within the thee larger stones. They seemed to be indistructable to him - and what angered him more so was that the runes themselves could still teleport without the vast stone structures. He scattered small fragments of the stones everywhere, hoping that without the larger pieces of the structure within their proximities, each piece of the devices would die.
Many did. The stones that we find at Torrin's Quarry were once pieces of the transporters which Arronax had buried deep underground. And due to the Technologichal nature of the devices that were used to mine for them, the stones lost all Magicke power and became mere rocks..}

{1724}{171 
However, many pieces are still active, yet they are hazardous. These smaller pieces have been known to send unwary travellers to unwanted destinations, such as crypts or tombs. Many believe that these were curses set upon the stones by the Dark Elf.

Nonetheless, the three integral rune-stones remain true to their purposes.

Written by Lord Haku.}

